   "insight": {
    "headline": "Crude jumped 5% within hours of the Hormuz attack",
    "body": "Brent rose 5.09% and WTI 4.95%, the sharpest single-day move in this digest's coverage, after a cargo vessel was struck by a projectile in the Strait of Hormuz. Gold added 1.96% and the dollar index slipped 0.29%. That combination - oil and gold up together, dollar down - is the classic signature of a geopolitical supply shock rather than an economic one. Equities went the other way, with the FTSE down 1.31% and the CAC 1.55%. For an Indian reader this is the most consequential number on the board: India imports the overwhelming majority of its crude through that waterway, and a 5% move feeds the import bill, the rupee at 95.66, and next month's inflation print."
   }
